# Chapter 2 · Colonial Perspectives of Gender

> "How did the colonial era influence the status of women in the subcontinent?" (2020). "Write a detailed note on the colonial and capitalistic perspectives of gender" (2023, and again in 2024 with the thesis that "development is the new name of coloniality"). This chapter covers the colonial half; Chapter 3 the capitalist half.

## 2.1  What a "colonial perspective of gender" means

A **colonial perspective of gender** is the way colonial rulers **saw, judged and changed** gender relations in the societies they ruled — and the way colonised peoples responded. It has three parts:

1. **The colonial gaze.** Colonisers described colonised women as **oppressed victims** of "backward" cultures, and colonised men as cruel or effeminate. This justified colonial rule as a "**civilising mission**".
2. **Colonial policies.** Laws, land settlements, education and economic changes that reshaped women's lives — sometimes improving them, often harming them.
3. **The nationalist response.** Local reformers and nationalists, in turn, used women as symbols of national identity and tradition.

::: simple
Colonialism did not find "tradition" and leave it alone. It **described** local women in a way that justified its rule, **changed** their legal and economic position, and **provoked** a nationalist reply that also used women as symbols.
:::

## 2.2  The colonial gaze: women as the measure of civilisation

- The British historian **James Mill** (*The History of British India*, ==1817==) argued that the condition of women is the measure of a society's civilisation, and judged Indian society low on this scale.
- The American writer **Katherine Mayo**'s *Mother India* (==1927==) portrayed Indian men as degenerate and women as helpless victims of child marriage and seclusion; it was used to argue that Indians were unfit for self-rule. Gandhi called it "a drain inspector's report".
- The theorist **Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak** ("Can the Subaltern Speak?", ==1988==) summed up the colonial attitude to *sati* as "**White men are saving brown women from brown men**". In the debate between British officials and Indian elites, the widow herself was never heard.
- The historian **Lata Mani** (*Contentious Traditions*, ==1998==) showed that in the *sati* debate women were "neither subjects nor objects but, rather, the **ground**" on which colonial and indigenous men argued about tradition.

## 2.3  Colonial laws and reforms in British India

::: table | Colonial laws that shaped women's lives in British India.
@widths 1.6,5.2,7.2
Year | Law or policy | Effect on women
==1829== | Bengal Sati Regulation | Banned widow burning after **Raja Ram Mohan Roy**'s campaign
==1856== | Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act | Allowed Hindu widows to remarry (Vidyasagar's campaign) — but they lost their late husband's property
==1860== | Indian Penal Code | A uniform criminal code; its adultery section (497) punished only the man and treated the wife as the husband's property
==1864–68== | Contagious Diseases and Cantonment Acts | Forced medical examination and registration of women suspected of prostitution near army bases
==1870== | Female Infanticide Prevention Act | Aimed at female infanticide in north India
==1871== | Criminal Tribes Act | Labelled whole communities, including *hijras* ("eunuchs"), as criminal
==1872== | Punjab Laws Act | Made **custom** the first rule in matters such as inheritance, so many Muslim women lost the shares Islamic law gave them
==1891== | Age of Consent Act | Raised the age of consent for girls from 10 to 12
==1929== | Child Marriage Restraint Act (Sarda Act) | Set minimum marriage ages
==1937 / 1939== | Shariat Application Act; Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act | Applied Muslim personal law; gave Muslim women grounds for judicial divorce
:::

## 2.4  How colonialism changed women's lives — four channels

### (1) Law: codifying "tradition"

The British ruled personal matters — marriage, divorce, inheritance — by **religious and customary law**, which they collected and **codified** with the help of male religious scholars and landowners. This turned flexible, varied practices into **rigid written rules**, often the most patriarchal versions. "**Anglo-Muhammadan law**" and the **Punjab Laws Act 1872**, which put custom before Islamic law, left many women in Punjab without the inheritance Islam allowed them.

### (2) Land and property: registering men

Colonial **land settlements** recorded land in the names of **male heads of households**. Women's customary rights of use — to graze animals, gather fuel, cultivate family plots — were not recorded and were easily lost. This laid the base of today's gap in land ownership.

### (3) Economy: new work, lost work

Colonial trade destroyed some crafts in which women worked (hand-spinning and weaving lost out to British machine cloth) and drew labour into **cash crops, plantations and army cantonments**. Men were drawn into wage work and the army; women's household and subsistence work lost value.

### (4) Education and reform

Missionaries and the colonial state opened some **girls' schools**; Indian reformers opened many more. Education created a small class of educated women who later led women's organisations — including APWA and the women of the Pakistan Movement (Topic 3).

::: fig mapping | Colonial policies and their effects on women.
@left Colonial policy
@right Effect on women
Codification of personal law => Rigid, patriarchal "tradition"
Punjab Laws Act 1872 (custom first) => Loss of Islamic inheritance shares
Land settlements in men's names => Loss of women's land rights
Cash crops and machine cloth => Loss of women's crafts and income
Reform laws (sati, remarriage, age of consent) => Some protection, on paper
Girls' schools => A small educated elite of women
:::

## 2.5  The nationalist response

The historian **Partha Chatterjee** ("The Nationalist Resolution of the Women's Question", ==1989==) argued that Indian nationalists divided life into an **outer, material world** (where the West could be copied) and an **inner, spiritual world** of home and family (which must stay "Indian"). Women became guardians of the inner world — educated, but modest and devoted to the home. This "**new woman**" was different from both the Western woman and the "backward" traditional woman.

Muslim reformers followed a similar path: **Nazir Ahmad**'s *Mirat-ul-Uroos* (==1869==) and **Mumtaz Ali**'s *Huquq-un-Niswan* (==1898==) promoted women's education **within** Islamic and family values. The result was a lasting link between women, **honour** and **national or religious identity**.

## 2.6  Assessment: did colonialism help or harm women?

::: balance | The colonial legacy for women in the subcontinent
- **Gains:** laws against *sati* and female infanticide; widow remarriage; age of consent and child-marriage laws; girls' schools; a legal basis for Muslim women's divorce (1939).
- **Losses:** codification of the most patriarchal readings of custom; loss of inheritance (Punjab 1872) and land rights; criminalisation of *hijras* (1871); control of women's bodies (Contagious Diseases Acts); use of women to justify colonial rule.
- **Limits of the gains:** reforms were few, often unenforced, and usually came from Indian reformers, not British initiative.
-> The colonial era **did not simply liberate or oppress** women. It **rearranged** patriarchy — replacing some local controls with new colonial ones — and left laws and property patterns Pakistan still lives with.
:::

## 2.7  The colonial legacy in Pakistan today

- The **Penal Code of 1860** remains the base of Pakistan's criminal law; parts of it have been amended many times.
- The **Child Marriage Restraint Act 1929** remained the national law until provinces and Islamabad raised the age (Sindh ==2013==; Islamabad ==2025==, age ==18==).
- Women's low **land ownership** and the denial of **inheritance** by custom echo colonial land records and the Punjab Laws Act.
- The treatment of ***khawaja siras*** carries the stigma of the **Criminal Tribes Act**.
- Debates over women as symbols of **national and religious identity** — in dress, the Aurat March, textbooks — continue the nationalist response.

::: eye | Answering 2020: "How did the colonial era influence the status of women?"
Use four channels as headings — **law, land, economy, education** — each with a dated law or policy and its effect. Add the **colonial gaze** (James Mill, Mayo, Spivak) and the **nationalist response** (Chatterjee). End with a balanced assessment and the legacy in Pakistan. Draw the mapping figure.
:::
